  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Calibration Certificate",
    "overview": "The report reads as a certificate issued by a test bench. The model is the instrument under test and the Campinas bulletin is the reference standard. Every figure is a reading checked against a tolerance band. The page is a single white sheet ruled with hairlines. A heavy ink rule opens it under the title block and closes it above the footer. Colors act as roles, not decoration: blue is the reference, ink is the instrument, yellow is the tolerance, and green and red are verdicts.\n\nThe page is dense in the way an instrument sheet is dense. It has a label/value spec grid, a readings table with Reference, Measured, Error, Tolerance and Verdict columns, then numbered figure plates with captions. Readings are mono and tabular. Words are set in Archivo, whose width narrows as the rank of a line rises. The system rejects the dark data-dashboard of tiles and the editorial long-read.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "White paper, blue-black ink, 1px hairline rules, and a 2px ink rule at the head and foot of the sheet.",
      "Color is a role: blue for the reference, ink for the instrument, yellow for tolerance, and green or red for verdicts.",
      "Archivo at varied widths for words. Geist Mono with tabular figures for every reading.",
      "Figures are numbered plates (\"Fig. N\") with a key and a caption. There are no cards and no tiles.",
      "Flat throughout. The only lifted object is the cursor readout."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Reference-Is-Blue Rule",
        "body": "Blue always means the bulletin, the reference standard. The model is always drawn in ink. Never swap them, and never use blue for a model series.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Yellow-Is-Tolerance Rule",
        "body": "Yellow appears only as a translucent acceptance band behind data (plus the selection highlight). It is never used for text, fills of marks, or emphasis.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Verdict-Only Rule",
        "body": "Green and red are judgments. They appear only where a reading has been compared with a tolerance.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Readings-Are-Mono Rule",
        "body": "Any number that is a measurement, count, date stamp or tolerance is set in Geist Mono with tabular figures. Words are never set in mono, except figure numbers and the code block.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Width-Is-Rank Rule",
        "body": "Higher rank means narrower Archivo: 78% for the title, 84% for figure titles, 88% for quantities and callouts, 90% for spec keys, and 100% for running text. Weight rises along with the narrowing.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ruled-Not-Raised Rule",
        "body": "Separation is always a hairline or an ink rule. Only an object that floats over data, like the readout, may cast a shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** draw every bulletin or reference series in Reference Blue (#0019f0) and every model series in Certificate Ink (#0b0e2a).",
      "**Do** show tolerance as a translucent yellow band (`rgba(244,222,2,.34)`) behind the data, and state the band's source in the caption.",
      "**Do** set every reading in Geist Mono with tabular figures, and right-align numeric columns.",
      "**Do** open and close the sheet with a 2px ink rule, and divide everything in between with 1px Hairline (#dfe2ee).",
      "**Do** number figures (\"Fig. N\", mono, Slate) and give each one a caption of at most 62ch.",
      "**Do** use `ink-2` or `ink-3` for secondary text on white. Both clear 4.5:1."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use green or red for anything that is not a verdict against a tolerance.",
      "**Don't** use yellow for text, marks, strokes or emphasis.",
      "**Don't** put readings in cards or tiles, and don't lift any surface except the floating readout.",
      "**Don't** use a dark ground. This world has `color-scheme: light` only.",
      "**Don't** pull the `web/` Tailwind tokens into this surface, or these tokens into `web/`.",
      "**Don't** use a place-ramp step lighter than `place-work` (#b9bdd4) as a data fill on white. Lighter steps fall below 3:1."
    ]
  }
}